\Section{SPHERICAL POLYGONS}

\Paragraph{319.}{Polygons and Triangles on a Sphere.} The portion of a
\index{Polygons@\textbf{Polygons}!spherical}
\index{Spherical@\textbf{Spherical}!polygon}
\index{Triangles@\textbf{Triangles}!symmetrical}
\begin{wrapfigure}[6]{r}{1.5in}
\hfill\smash{\raisebox{-1in}{\Input{128a}{1.5in}}}
\end{wrapfigure}
spherical surface contained within a
polyhedral angle whose vertex is at the
center of a sphere is called a \emph{spherical
polygon}.

It follows that for every spherical
polygon there is a corresponding polyhedral
angle at the center of the sphere
made by drawing radii to the vertices
of the polygon. The face angles of the polyhedral angle correspond
to the sides of the spherical polygon and the dihedral
angles of the polyhedral angle to the angles of the spherical
polygon.

Since a plane through the center of a sphere intersects the
surface 
\begin{wrapfigure}[7]{r}{1.5in}
\hfill\smash{\raisebox{-1.125in}{\Input{128b}{1.5in}}}
\end{wrapfigure}
in a great circle, it follows that the sides of a spherical
polygon are \emph{arcs of great circles}.

\Indent Since a plane may be passed through
the vertex of a polyhedral angle such
that the polyhedral angle lies entirely
on one side of it, it follows that a
spherical polygon \emph{lies within one hemisphere}.

A spherical polygon of three sides is a \emph{spherical triangle}.

\Paragraph{320.}{Relation between the Parts of a Spherical Polygon and
the Corresponding Polyhedral Angle.}
\index{Corresponding@\textbf{Corresponding}!polyhedral angles}

(1)~The face angles of the polyhedral angle are measured by
the arcs forming the sides of the spherical polygon. Why?

(2)~The dihedral angles of the polyhedral angle are equal
in measure to the angles of the spherical polygon.\hfill §~\Sref{318}

