\Section{SYMMETRICAL TRIANGLES ARE EQUAL IN AREA}

\Paragraph{354.}{Equal Areas Defined.} Two spherical polygons are said to
\index{Equal@\textbf{Equal}!areas}
\index{Triangles@\textbf{Triangles}!equal}
have equal areas if they can be made to coincide, or if they can
be divided into parts which can be made to coincide in pairs.

\begin{Remark}
Compare this with the definition of equal areas in Plane Geometry.
\end{Remark}

\begin{Theorem}{355.}{XIX.}
Two symmetrical spherical
triangles are equal in area.
\end{Theorem}

\begin{center}
\Input{142a}{0.4\textwidth}
\end{center}

\Proof: Let $ABC$ be one of the given triangles. Extend the
radii $AO$, $BO$, $CO$ to meet the sphere in $A_1$, $B_1$, $C_1$, thus forming
a triangle symmetrical to $\triangle ABC$. \hfill §~\Sref{327}

Let $P$ be a pole of the circle through $A$, $B$, $C$. Extend $PO$
to meet the sphere in $P_1$. Draw $\wideparen{PA}$, $\wideparen{PB}$, $\wideparen{PC}$, and
$\wideparen{P_1A_1}$, $\wideparen{P_1B_1}$,
and $\wideparen{P_1C_1}$.

Suppose that $P$ lies within $\triangle ABC$.

The spherical triangles $PAB$, $PBC$, $PCA$, $P_1A_1B_1$, $P_1B_1C_1$,
$P_1C_1A_1$ are all isosceles.\hfill §~\Sref{296}

Now prove
\begin{gather*}
(1) \triangle PAB = \triangle P_1A_1B_1; \qquad (2) \triangle PBC = \triangle P_1B_1C_1; \\
(3) \triangle PCA = \triangle P_1C_1A_1. \\
\therefore\quad \text{area } \triangle ABC = \text{area } \triangle A_1B_1C_1.
\end{gather*}

But $\triangle A_1B_1C_1$ is equal to any other triangle which is symmetrical
to $\triangle ABC$.

$\therefore$ Two symmetrical spherical triangles are equal in area.


\Section{BIRECTANGULAR SPHERICAL TRIANGLES}

\Paragraph{356.}{A birectangular spherical triangle} is one having two right
\index{Birectangular@\textbf{Birectangular spherical triangle}}
\index{Triangles@\textbf{Triangles}!birectangular}
angles, as $\triangle PAB$ in the figure.

If the third angle of a birectangular triangle
is~$1$°, the triangle contains one of $720$ equal
parts of the surface of the sphere.

\noindent\begin{minipage}[b]{\textwidth-1.5in}
\Indent\Paragraph{357.}{Spherical Degree.} The area of a birectangular
\index{Degree@\textbf{Degree}!spherical}
\index{Spherical@\textbf{Spherical}!degree, minute, second}
triangle having a vertex angle of one
degree is called a \emph{spherical degree} and is used
as a unit of measure of areas of spherical polygons.

\Indent In a similar manner we define a \emph{spherical minute} and a
\emph{spherical second}.
\end{minipage}
\hfil\Input{143a}{1.25in}\hfil

\BeforeSkip
\noindent\begin{minipage}[b]{\textwidth-1.5in}
\Indent\Paragraph{358.}{The Lune.} A \emph{lune} is a figure formed
\index{Lune@\textbf{Lune}!angle of}
by two great semicircles having the same end-points.
The angle between these semicircles
is the \emph{angle of the lune}.

\Indent\begin{Corollary}{359.}{1.}
The area of a birectangular
spherical triangle in terms of spherical degrees
is equal to the number of degrees in the
third angle of the triangle.
\end{Corollary}
\end{minipage}
\hfil\Input{143b}{1.25in}\hfil

\begin{Corollary}{360.}{2.}
The area of a lune in terms of spherical
degrees is twice the number of degrees in the angle of the lune.
\end{Corollary}

\Paragraph{361.}{Spherical Excess.} The number of degrees by which
the sum of the angles of a spherical triangle exceeds $180$° is
called the \emph{spherical excess} of the triangle.
\index{Spherical@\textbf{Spherical}!excess}

The spherical excess of a spherical polygon is the sum of its
angles less $(n - 2)180$°, where $n$ is the number of sides of the
polygon.

