\Section{AREA OF A SPHERICAL TRIANGLE}
\SecToc{Area of the Sphere}

\begin{Theorem}{362.}{XX.}
\index{Area@\textbf{Area}!of a spherical triangle}
\index{Measurement@\textbf{Measurement}!of surfaces}
The area of a spherical triangle
in terms of spherical degrees is equal to its spherical
excess.\footnotemark
\end{Theorem}
\footnotetext{This theorem was discovered by Cavalieri. See \hyperref[page:Cavalieri]{Frontispiece}.}
\Page{134}

\begin{center}
\Input{144a}{0.4\textwidth}
\end{center}

\Proof: We are to show that area $\triangle ABC = \angle A + \angle B + \angle C
- 180$°.

By §~\Sref{360}, the areas of the lunes $ACDB$, $CAEB$, $BCFA$ in
spherical degrees are as follows:
\begin{align*}
\triangle ABC + \triangle BCD &= ACDB = 2 \angle A. \\
\triangle ABC + \triangle BAE &= CAEB = 2 \angle C. \\
\triangle ABC + \triangle CFA &= BCFA = 2 \angle B.
\end{align*}

Hence, adding,

$3\triangle ABC + \triangles BCD,\ BAE,\ CFA = 2(\angle A + \angle B + \angle C)$.

Now $\triangles BCD$ and $AEF$ are symmetrical and equal in area.

Hence, \\
$2 \triangle ABC + \triangles ABC,\ AEF,\ BAE,\ CFA = 2(\angle A + \angle B + \angle C)$.

But $\triangles ABC,\ AEF,\ BAE,\ CFA$ together constitute a hemisphere
or $360$~spherical degrees.

Hence, $2 \triangle ABC + 360\text{°} = 2 (\angle A + \angle B + \angle C)$.

Solving, $\triangle ABC = \angle A + \angle B + \angle C - 180$°.
\QED

\medskip
\noindent\hrule
\medskip

\textsc{Note}.---The spherical degree differs fundamentally from the units
of measure hitherto used. This unit is a certain fraction of the surface
of the sphere and hence its actual size depends upon the size of the
sphere.


\Section{AREA OF A SPHERICAL POLYGON}

\begin{Theorem}{363.}{XXI.}
\index{Area@\textbf{Area}!of a spherical polygon}
The area of a spherical polygon
in terms of spherical degrees is equal to its spherical
excess.
\end{Theorem}

\Proof: Join one vertex of the polygon to each non-adjacent
vertex, thus forming $n - 2$ spherical triangles. Now since the
sum of the spherical excesses of these triangles is the spherical
excess of the polygon, the conclusion is evident.

