\Section{LATERAL AREA OF A FRUSTUM OF A CONE}

\begin{Theorem}{364.}{XXII.}
The lateral area of a frustum
of a right circular cone is equal to the altitude of
the frustum multiplied by the circumference of a circle
whose radius is the perpendicular distance from a point
in the axis of the frustum to the middle point of an
element.
\end{Theorem}

\begin{center}
\Input{146ab}{}
\end{center}

\Objective{Given a frustum with an element $AA'$ whose middle point is $B$,
$CC'$ the axis of the frustum, $BD \perp CC'$ and $EB \perp AA'$.}
{To prove that the lateral area is equal to $2\pi\cdot EB\cdot CC'$.}

\Proof: By §~\Sref{269}, the lateral area is $2\pi\cdot BD\cdot AA'$.

Hence, we must show that
\[
EB \cdot CC' = BD \cdot AA'.
\]

\noindent\begin{minipage}[b]{\textwidth-1.25in}
\Indent To do this, draw $A'F \perp AC$ and show that
$\triangle AFA' \sim \triangle EDB$.

\Indent\begin{Corollary}{365.}{}
The lateral area of a right
circular cone is equal to its altitude times the length
of a circle whose radius is the perpendicular from
a point in the axis to the middle point of an element.
\end{Corollary}
\end{minipage}
\hfil\Input{146c}{1in}\hfil

{\small\textit{Note}. The above theorem and corollary are needed in deducing the
area of the surface of a sphere. We have already computed the area of a
spherical triangle in terms of \emph{spherical degrees}, but we now wish to derive
the area of the spherical surface in terms of \emph{plane units of measure}.}


\Section{CIRCUMSCRIBED AND INSCRIBED CONES AND FRUSTUMS}

\noindent\begin{minipage}[b]{\textwidth-1.5in}
\Indent\Paragraph{366.}{} About a circle circumscribe a polygon as follows: Construct
\index{Circumscribed@\textbf{Circumscribed}!cone}
\index{Generator@\textbf{Generator}!of a spherical surface}
\index{Inscribed@\textbf{Inscribed}!cone}
two diameters $AB$ and $CD$ at right angles to each other
and divide each quadrant into an \emph{even} number of parts by
points, as $E$, $F$, $G$. At each alternate
division point, beginning with the first
point~$E$, draw a tangent.

\Indent There results a regular polygon with
two vertices on each of the diameters
$AB$ and $CD$ extended.
\end{minipage}
\hfill\Input{147a}{1.5in}

If now we construct another polygon
in the same manner by dividing each
quadrant into \emph{twice} as many arcs, it will
likewise have two vertices on each of
the diameters $AB$ and $CD$ extended. The vertices on $AB$ will
lie between $M$ and~$N$.

This construction may be repeated at pleasure, thus obtaining
a sequence of polygons each lying closer to the circle than
the preceding.

Now inscribe a polygon similar to the first one of the set just
circumscribed, by joining the points $C$ and $F$, $F$ and $A$, and so on,
repeating this process to form a sequence of inscribed polygons.

If now the whole figure is made to revolve about $AB$ as an
axis, the circle generates a sphere, and the circumscribed and
inscribed polygons generate sets of circumscribed and inscribed
cones and frustums of cones.

\Paragraph{367.}{Fundamental Assumption on the Area of a Sphere.} We
assume that
\index{Axioms@\textbf{Axioms}}
\index{Measurement@\textbf{Measurement}!of surfaces}

\textit{A sphere has a definite area which is less than the surface of
any circumscribed figure and greater than the surface of any
inscribed convex figure}.

\begin{Remark}
The student should note that while the statement just preceding is
obviously true, it is not capable of proof by pure deduction. It is therefore
\emph{necessarily} in the nature of an \emph{assumption} or \emph{axiom}.
\end{Remark}


\Section{FORMULA FOR THE AREA OF A SPHERE}

\begin{Theorem}{368.}{XXIII.}
\index{Area@\textbf{Area}!of a sphere}
\index{Sphere@\textbf{Sphere}!area of}
The area of a sphere whose
radius is $r$ is $4\pi r^2$.
\end{Theorem}

\noindent\begin{minipage}[b]{\textwidth-1.75in}
\Indent\Proof: (a)~Denote by $r$ the radius of the circle which generates
the sphere. That is, in the figure, $r = OG = OE = OH$.

\Indent By §~\Sref{364} the lateral areas of the outside frustums whose axes
are $OL$ and $OK$ are $2\pi r\cdot OL$ and $2\pi r\cdot OK$, and by §~\Sref{365} the
lateral areas of the cones whose axes are $LN$ and $KM$ are respectively
$2\pi r\cdot LN$ and $2\pi r\cdot KM$.
\end{minipage}
\hfil\Input{148a}{1.5in}\hfil

Hence, the total surface of the whole circumscribed figure is
\begin{flalign*}
&&          &2\pi r\cdot MK + 2\pi r\cdot KO + 2\pi r\cdot OL + 2\pi r\cdot LN, && \\
&\text{or}& &2\pi r (MK + KO + OL + LN) = 2\pi r\cdot MN. &&
\end{flalign*}

If now a polygon of twice the number of sides is constructed,
as described in §~\Sref{366}, we obtain another circumscribed
figure whose area is $2\pi r\cdot M'N'$ where $M'$ and $N'$ are the two
vertices on the line $AB$ extended, but lying between $M$ and $N$,
so that $2\pi r\cdot M'N' < 2\pi r\cdot MN$.

As this process goes on, the total surface generated decreases
and may be made to approximate \emph{as nearly as we please} to
\[
2\pi r × AB = 2\pi r × 2r = 4\pi r^2.
\]
Therefore, the area of the sphere cannot be greater than $4\pi r^2$.


(b)~Using the same figure, let $OG'$ be the apothem of the
inscribed polygon.

Then, as under~(a), we find that the area of the figure developed
by revolving the inscribed polygon about $AB$ is
\[
2\pi \cdot AB \cdot OG' = 4\pi r \cdot OG'.
\]
By continuing to double the number of sides, $OG'$ may be made
to approach as nearly as we please to $OG = r$, $OG'$ being
always less than $OG$.

Hence, the total surface developed increases and approaches
\emph{as nearly as we please to}
\[
4\pi r × OG = 4\pi r^2.
\]

Therefore the area of the sphere cannot be less than $4\pi r^2$.

Since from (a) and~(b) the area of the surface of the sphere
can be neither greater than $4\pi r^2$ nor less than $4\pi r^2$, it follows
that it is exactly equal to $4\pi r^2$.
\QED

