\Section{THE VOLUME OF A SPHERE BY INSPECTION}
\SecToc{Volume of the Sphere}

\noindent\begin{minipage}[b]{\textwidth-1.5in}
\Indent\Paragraph{369.}{} The formula for the volume of a sphere may be inferred
directly from the accompanying figure.
\index{Sphere@\textbf{Sphere}!volume of}
\index{Volume@\textbf{Volume}!of a sphere}
\index{Measurement@\textbf{Measurement}!of volumes}

The sphere is covered with a network
of spherical quadrilaterals. If these are
taken small enough, they may be regarded
as approximately \emph{plane surfaces}.
\end{minipage}
\hfil\Input{151a}{1.5in}\hfil

On this supposition we have a set of
pyramids with a common altitude~$r$ and
the sum of their bases approximately
equal to the area of the sphere.

Hence, their combined volume is $\frac{1}{3} r × (\text{area of sphere})$
or $\tfrac{1}{3} r \cdot 4\pi r^2$. That is, the volume is $\tfrac{4}{3} \pi r^3$.

It is clear that, by making these quadrilaterals sufficiently
small each one may be made to approach as nearly as we
please to a plane surface. Hence, each pyramidal figure with
vertex at $O$ is made to approach the form of a true pyramid
as nearly as we please.

\Paragraph{370.}{Fundamental Assumption on the Volume and Surface of a
Sphere.} In the formal proof on page~\Pgref{142}, we use polyhedrons
circumscribed about the sphere and we assume that
\index{Axioms@\textbf{Axioms}}

\textit{The surface and the volume of a sphere may be approximated
as nearly as we please by taking the surfaces and the volumes
of a series of circumscribed polyhedrons all of whose faces are
made to decrease indefinitely}.

\begin{Remark}
\textit{Note}. This assumption, when taken together with §~\Sref{367}, implies that
the surface obtained by taking the surfaces of a series of polyhedrons
is the same as the surface obtained by taking the surfaces of the circumscribed
figures described in §~\Sref{366}. This is of course obvious at a glance,
though a formal deductive proof is very difficult.
\end{Remark}

