\Section{SPHERICAL SEGMENTS, CONES, SECTORS}

\Paragraph{372.}{Zone, Segment.} That part of a spherical surface included
\index{Altitude@\textbf{Altitude}!of a zone}
\index{Segment@\textbf{Segment}!spherical}
\index{Spherical@\textbf{Spherical}!segment}
\index{Zone@\textbf{Zone}!altitude of}
\index{Zone@\textbf{Zone}!bases of}
\index{Zone@\textbf{Zone}!of one base}
between two parallel planes cutting it is called a
\emph{zone}. The perpendicular distance between the planes is the
\emph{altitude} of the zone and of the corresponding segment.

The portion of a sphere included between two parallel
planes cutting it is called a \emph{spherical segment}, and the two
circular sections made by the parallel planes are its \emph{bases}.
\index{Altitude@\textbf{Altitude}!of a spherical segment}
\index{Base@\textbf{Base}!of a spherical segment}

If one of the cutting planes is tangent to the sphere, then
the spherical segment and the corresponding zone are said to
have but \emph{one base}. The altitude in this case is the perpendicular
distance from the base to the tangent plane.

\noindent\begin{minipage}[b]{\textwidth-1.375in}
\Indent\Paragraph{373.}{Spherical Cone, Sector.} If one nappe
\index{Base@\textbf{Base}!of a spherical sector}
\index{Cone@\textbf{Cone}!spherical}
\index{Sector@\textbf{Sector}!spherical}
\index{Spherical@\textbf{Spherical}!cone}
\index{Spherical@\textbf{Spherical}!sector}
of a convex conical surface has its vertex
at the center of a sphere, the portion of
the sphere cut out by this surface is called
a \emph{spherical cone}.

\Indent If two spherical cones have the same axis,
one lying within the other, the figure formed
by their two lateral surfaces, together with
the part of the sphere intercepted between
them, is called a \emph{spherical sector}.

If the two cones are right circular cones, they
intercept circles on the sphere, and the zone thus
included is called the \emph{base} of the spherical sector.
\end{minipage}
\hfill\Input{154ab}{1.25in}

\BeforeSkip
